<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Tysilio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Tysilio was a Welsh saint of the seventh century, said in tradition to be a son of Brochwel Ysgithrog, king of Powys. He is associated above all with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meifod">Meifod</a>, the mother church of Powys, and churches dedicated to him are found across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, including Llandysilio on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menai-strait">Menai Strait</a>. His feast day is 8 November.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-tysilio.jpg" alt="Saint Tysilio" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Édouard Hue (EdouardHue) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buste_reliquaire_de_saint_Suliac,_Sizun,_France.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tradition makes Tysilio the second son of Brochwel Ysgithrog, King of Powys, and his wife Arddun Penasgell, and a nephew on his mother's side of Dunod, abbot of Bangor Iscoed. He is also known as, or confused with, Saint Suliac, and his name appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Tysilius or Suliacus. He took part in the affairs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in the troubled years at the opening of the 7th century and is said to have died in 640.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tysilio probably began his career at Trallwng Llywelyn, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welshpool">Welshpool</a>, and then settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meifod">Meifod</a>, where he studied under Gwyddfarch. Around 630 he moved to an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menai-strait">Menai Strait</a>, now called Ynys Tysilio, set up a hermitage there and preached across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Ynys Môn</a> for about seven years. Returning to Meifod, he talked the ageing abbot out of a pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and in time succeeded Gwyddfarch as abbot.</p><p>King Brochwel, who spent his summers hunting in the Vale of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meifod">Meifod</a> and often visited the shrine of Saint Gwyddfarch during his stays at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathrafal">Mathrafal</a>, gave his son the bishopric of that part of his kingdom. Tysilio founded the second church at Meifod, Eglwys Tysilio. His feast day was also the patronal festival and wakes of the nearby parish of Guilsfield, where a holy well, the Fons Tysilio, was dedicated to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Tradition names Tysilio as the original author of the Brut Tysilio, a version of the Welsh chronicle Brut y Brenhinedd. Brynley F. Roberts showed, however, that it took shape around 1500 as an amalgam of earlier versions of the Brut y Brenhinedd, which itself derives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a>'s 12th-century Latin Historia Regum Britanniae.</p><p>His name survives in Llandysilio in Powys, Llandissilio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembrokeshire">Pembrokeshire</a>, Llandysiliogogo in Ceredigion and Llantysilio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbighshire">Denbighshire</a>, and across the border in Herefordshire in the Church of St Tysilio at Sellack and in Llancillo Church. It also forms part of Llanfairpwllgwyngyllgogerychwyrndrobwllllantysiliogogogoch, the longest place name in the United Kingdom, although that name was invented in the late 19th century for the growing tourist trade.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tysilio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Meifod in the Vyrnwy valley, associated with <b>Saint Tysilio</b>, was the principal church of the kingdom and a royal burial place, and many small churches bore the names of local saints.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
